<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hu Shi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1891–1962</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hu Shi was a Chinese philosopher, essayist and diplomat, a student of John Dewey and a leader of the New Culture Movement. His essays of 1917 called for literature written in the vernacular rather than classical Chinese, a change that transformed Chinese writing. He served as ambassador to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> from 1938 to 1942 and later presided over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/academia-sinica">Academia Sinica</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hu-shi.jpg" alt="Hu Shi" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hu_Shih_1960_color_Crop.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
